summary:
  The retry switch also gates the resume that follows the network coming back,
  which is the one recovery the listener asked to keep.

history:
  The distinction is the whole point of the setting. "Disable all resume unless
  an actual network connection was dropped" — a stream dying while the link is
  fine is the unwanted case; a link that went away and came back is the
  expected one. The widget can tell them apart because they arrive on
  different roads: the ladder from a playback failure, the resume from
  QNetworkInformation's reachability edge. Gating both would be easy to do by
  accident while tidying, and would quietly take away the half that was never
  in question.

target:
  package/contents/ui/main.qml

find:
              if (!root._wantsPlaying || isPlaying() || root._casting) return;
              if (_orderSubject() === null) return;
              console.log("[ARP] network is back — resuming the standing order");

replace:
              if (!root._wantsPlaying || isPlaying() || root._casting) return;
              if (Plasmoid.configuration.autoRetry !== true) return;
              console.log("[ARP] network is back — resuming the standing order");

expect:
  Killed by tests/test_invariants.py::test_the_retry_switch_gates_the_ladder_and_not_the_network.
  Line-neutral.
